.TH FPACMD5 1 "23 November 2026" Linux "FPAC Operator's Manual"
.SH NAME
fpacmd5 \- MD5 answer to the SYSop challenge of an FPAC node
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fpacmd5
[\-n] [\-l fr|en]
.RI [ challenge ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
The
.B SYSop
command of
.BR fpacnode (1)
shows a challenge such as
.IP
F6BVP-12> 3 7 1 12 5 [1795430000]
.LP
.B fpacmd5
prints the MD5 answer to it: the MD5 of the number in brackets, as shown,
followed by the sysop password
.RB ( Password
in
.BR fpac.conf (5)).
Paste that answer in the session. It changes with every challenge and
reveals nothing of the password, unlike the answer with the characters at
the 5 positions, which shows 5 of them to whoever listens.
.LP
The challenge may be given on the command line (in quotes, because of the
brackets), whole or as the number in brackets alone; otherwise it is
asked for. The password is then asked without echo and is written
nowhere. When the standard input is not a terminal, the password is read
from its first line.
.LP
.B fpacmd5
uses the MD5 code of
.B fpacnode
itself. It is meant to be run by the sysop on the machine he connects
from; it needs no AX.25 library.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 12
.B \-n
Also print the answer with the characters at the 5 positions (needs the
whole challenge line).
.TP 12
.B "\-l fr|en"
Language of the messages, French or English. By default, after
LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES, LANGUAGE or LANG.
.TP 12
.B \-h
Print the usage and exit.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
$ fpacmd5 'F6BVP-12> 3 7 1 12 5 [1795430000]'
Sysop password:
74dd087233db623217dac114659be142
.fi
.LP
(with the password "mypassword").
.SH NOTES
A node running FPAC older than 4.2-rc5 on a 64-bit system works out a
wrong MD5 and refuses this answer: use the 5 characters there
.RB ( \-n ).
Since 4.2-rc5 the password itself, typed in plain text, is refused.
.SH SEE ALSO
fpacnode(1), fpac.conf(5)
.SH AUTHOR
Bernard Pidoux F6BVP <bernard.f6bvp@gmail.com>
